def power_width():
    return show(
        r"Two address chunks of $e=m^k$ radix-$q$ digits each are interchanged in time $O(Ve^\tau)$, provided a row field preceding both has length divisible by $W^k$.",
        "Split rows into $W$ role streams, run the bit network with each edge applied as a shear whose pivots are interchanges of $e/m$-digit fields (smaller calls of the same procedure), then finish with $D\\leftarrow D-H$, $H\\leftarrow H+D$, $D\\leftarrow H-D$; solve the resulting recurrence.",
        mot("The sum of the edge ranks is below the number of wires times $m$, so the recurrence saves a power of the chunk width.",
            "explicit", (1350, r"We use the bit network to interchange two equal address chunks on tape.",
                         r"the resulting recurrence saves a power of the chunk width.")),
        [
            bit_shear(),
            lower_tri(),
            matrix_shear(),
            infer(r"For $k=0$ a single-digit interchange costs $O(V)$ by the elementary stream operations.",
                  1492, r"For $k=0$ apply the single-digit operation of"),
            infer(r"Splitting whole rows cyclically into $W$ role streams gives streams of the same shape, each of volume $V/W$ and with row count divisible by $W^{k-1}$.",
                  1497, r"Split whole rows cyclically into $W$ role streams within each prefix", r"divisible by $W^{k-1}$."),
            infer(r"On every circuit edge apply $\Phi_{A_e}$ to that role stream by the matrix-shear lemma, recursively interchanging each pivot pair of $b$-digit fields, and run each gate pointwise on matching addresses.",
                  1508, r"On every circuit edge apply", r"matching addresses of its role streams."),
            infer(r"A common address permutation commutes with every pointwise bit gate, so the frame identity applies with frames $\Phi_M$.",
                  1517, r"The same invariant proof applies", r"pointwise bit gate."),
            infer(r"The physical output on role $\rho(w)$ is $\Phi_{M_{\mathrm{out}(\rho(w))}}\Phi_{-M_{\mathrm{in}(w)}}f_w=\Phi_{I_m}f_w$ by the endpoint condition $M_{\mathrm{out}(\rho(w))}-M_{\mathrm{in}(w)}=I_m$.",
                  1521, r"The logical circuit routes $g_w$ to role $\rho(w)$.", r"by~\eqref{eq:finite-shear-contract}."),
            infer(r"Merging rows back undoes $\rho$ and restores row order, leaving $H\leftarrow H+D$ on every original row, including rows assigned to scratch roles whose initial values were arbitrary.",
                  1529, r"Merge by returning row", r"whose initial values were arbitrary."),
            infer(r"The updates $D\leftarrow D-H$, $H\leftarrow H+D$, $D\leftarrow H-D$ send $(H,D)$ to $(D,H)$; only the middle shear uses the circuit and the other two target the later group at linear cost.",
                  1535, r"To interchange the two full chunks, perform", r"sends $(H,D)$ to $(D,H)$."),
            show(
                r"With $F_k$ the time divided by logical volume for width $m^k$, $F_0=O(1)$ and $F_k\le(s/W)F_{k-1}+O(1)$, hence $F_k=O(e^\tau)$.",
                "Count the recursive calls (exactly $s$, each on volume $V/W$), write the recurrence for the normalized cost, and sum the geometric series in the cases $s/W\\le1$ and $s/W>1$.",
                mot("Each of the $s$ calls acts on a stream of only $1/W$ of the volume, so dividing by the current volume multiplies the child cost by $s/W$ rather than $s$.", "recoverable"),
                [
                    infer(r"The number of recursive calls is the sum of the edge ranks, exactly $s$, each acting on one role stream of volume $V/W$ with all other fields as spectators.",
                          1543, r"The number of recursive calls is the sum of the edge ranks, exactly $s$.", r"as spectators."),
                    infer(r"Splitting, merging, gates and triangular operations cost $O(V)$ at the node because their number is fixed.",
                          1545, r"Splitting, merging, gates, and triangular operations have total cost $O(V)$ because",
                          r"their number is fixed."),
                    infer(r"The normalized cost satisfies $F_0=O(1)$ and $F_k\le(s/W)F_{k-1}+O(1)$.",
                          1548, r"Thus if $F_k$ is a", r"F_k\le(s/W)F_{k-1}+O(1)."),
                    infer(r"For $a=s/W<m^\tau$ the sum $\sum_{j=0}^ka^j$ is $O(m^{k\tau})$: at most $k+1$ if $a\le1$ and $O(a^k)$ if $a>1$; hence $F_k=O(e^\tau)$.",
                          1554, r"For $a=s/W<m^\tau$ the geometric sum", r"Hence $F_k=O(e^\tau)$."),
                ],
                frm=["recursion-and-divide-and-conquer-recurrences", "summation-geometric-series-and-products", "power-saving-recurrence"]),
            show(
                r"A depth-first schedule on a fixed number of tapes adds only $O(V)$ at each node, even when the payload is one bit.",
                "Park the inactive role streams on a stack, reuse the same role tapes for the child, and bound descriptor work by a power of $\\log V$ using the invariant that both chunk ranges stay present at every depth.",
                mot("Temporary storage must not be counted as logical volume in the factor $s/W$, so the schedule's own cost is charged to the parent's volume.", "recoverable"),
                [
                    infer(r"The machine reserves $W$ role tapes, a fixed set of work tapes, one parking stack tape and a separate descriptor stack.",
                          1561, r"Reserve $W$ role tapes, a fixed set of", r"separate descriptor stack."),
                    infer(r"Before a recursive call the $W-1$ inactive role streams are appended to the parking stack, the active stream is copied to the child area and the parent role tapes are erased.",
                          1568, r"Before a recursive call, append the $W-1$ inactive role streams to the", r"erase the parent role tapes while traversing"),
                    infer(r"Each push, pop, copy or erasure moves $O(V)$ symbols at a parent node, and there are only the fixed number $s$ of child calls, giving the additional $O(V)$ term.",
                          1584, r"Each push, pop, copy, positioning operation, or erasure moves $O(V)$", r"even though each child itself has volume $V/W$."),
                    infer(r"With a one-bit payload, both chunk ranges stay present, so $V_j\ge q^{2e_0}$ and $j\log W=O(\log V_j)$, hence $\log V_0=O(\log(2V_j))$.",
                          1595, r"Both original chunk ranges remain present:", r"=O(\log(2V_j))$."),
                    infer(r"Descriptor processing therefore takes $(\log(2V_0))^{O(1)}=O(V_j)$ time at each child.",
                          1606, r"$(\log(2V_0))^{O(1)}=O(V_j)$ time at that child,", r"is $O(V_j)$."),
                ],
                frm=["worst-case-running-time-and-subroutines", "fixed-tape-model", "stream-operations"]),
        ],
        page="power-width-interchange",
        frm=["stream-operations", "controlled-shifts", "frames", "power-saving-recurrence", "fixed-tape-model",
             "recursion-and-divide-and-conquer-recurrences"],
        adds=[bind(r"H", "high chunk", "the address chunk written as a vector of $m$ fields that is moved by the shear", "direct"),
              bind(r"D", "low chunk", "the second address chunk, a vector of $m$ fields, that controls the shear", "direct"),
              bind(r"e", "chunk width", "the number of radix-$q$ digits in each interchanged chunk, $e=m^k$", "count"),
              bind(r"k", "recursion depth", "the number of recursion levels, with $e=m^k$", "count")],
        salient="The exponent $\\tau$ is the same at every width $m^k$: each level multiplies the cost by $s/W<m$ while the width grows by the factor $m$, so the sum is dominated by the deepest level.")


def arbitrary_width():
    return show(
        r"Exchanging two disjoint contiguous address fields of $u$ bits, with any gaps and even a one-bit payload, costs $O(Vu^\tau)$ for the fixed $\tau=1-2^{-50}<1$.",
        "Peel a few high digits off each chunk to supply the rows the power-width procedure needs, pad the row count, split the remaining width in base $m$, interchange each piece, then undo the peeling and padding.",
        mot("Rows for a general array can be supplied by a few of its own high digits, and their movement costs less than the final bound.",
            "explicit", (1627, r"We have proved the fast operation for power widths with enough rows.", r"Their movement will cost less than the final bound.")),
        [
            power_width(),
            infer(r"For width $e$ digits put $k=\lceil\log_me\rceil$ and peel $\rho=\lceil k\log W/(2\log q)\rceil$ high digits, which is $O(\log(2e))$; the bounded set of small widths is handled by single-digit interchanges.",
                  1650, r"First suppose the two ranges are $[q^e]$,", r"individual digit"),
            infer(r"The high digits are interchanged one pair at a time and the digits of $h^+$ moved to sit after $d^+$, so the first two fields form a row field of range $R=q^{2\rho}\ge W^k$ before both remaining chunks.",
                  1677, r"In the two middle orders, the first two fields form a row field of range", r"before both remaining chunks."),
            infer(r"Padding with zero rows to $R'=W^k\lceil R/W^k\rceil<2R$ makes the row count divisible by every needed $W^j$ and less than doubles the volume.",
                  1684, r"Append zero rows, separately within", r"The padded logical volume is less than $2V$."),
            infer(r"Writing $e-\rho=\sum_j a_jm^j$ with $0\le a_j<m$, each of the $a_j$ pieces of width $m^j$ is interchanged by the power-width procedure on the same row field, costing $O(V)\sum_ja_jm^{j\tau}=O(Ve^\tau)$.",
                  1692, r"Write the remaining width in base $m$:", r"O(V)\sum_j a_jm^{j\tau}=O(Ve^\tau)."),
            infer(r"Because $a_j<m$ and $m^k<me$ the sum is geometric of the stated order; unpadding and undoing the high-digit joins cost $O(V\log(2e))=O(Ve^\tau)$.",
                  1707, r"The fixed bound $a_j<m$ and the inequality $m^k<me$ make this a", r"is $O(Ve^\tau)$."),
            infer(r"There are at most $(m-1)(k+1)=O(\log(2e))$ pieces, so their descriptor work is a fixed power of $\log(2V)$ and hence $O(V)$.",
                  1714, r"Computing the piece list and every new shape is polynomial in", r"hence $O(V)$."),
            infer(r"For binary chunks of $u$ bits, pad each range from $[2^u]$ to $[q^e]$ with $q^e\ge2^u$ minimal, increasing volume by less than the fixed factor $q^2$, and scan away invalid entries afterwards; since $e=O(u)$ the bound is $O(Vu^\tau)$.",
                  1721, r"Finally, for binary chunks let $e$ be the least integer with", r"the bound is $O(Vu^\tau)$."),
            infer(r"For chunks whose widths differ by one bit, peel the extra leading bit, interchange the equal-width parts and move the bit, adding one linear-cost single-bit move.",
                  1741, r"For widths differing by one bit, choose the leading bit of the longer", r"chunk as the extra bit."),
        ],
        page="arbitrary-width-interchange",
        frm=["power-width-interchange", "stream-operations", "logarithms-exponentials-floor-and-ceiling"],
        adds=[bind(r"u", "field width", "the number of bits in each of the two interchanged address fields", "count"),
              bind(r"\rho", "peeled digits", "the number of high digits taken off each chunk to serve as the row field", "count")],
        salient="The exponent $\\tau$ holds for every width $u$, not only widths $m^k$: the base-$m$ expansion of the width gives a geometric sum dominated by the largest piece.")


def packed_bits():
    return show(
        r"A binary row addition $y_{j_i}\leftarrow y_{j_i}\oplus x_{j_i}$ at the selected bit positions $j_i=\rho+iK$ ($0\le i<f$) of whole chunks of width $L=fK$ costs $O(V(L^\tau+1))$, restoring a third chunk of arbitrary contents.",
        "Do all $f$ XORs as a constant number of cyclic shifts of whole chunk ranges, with guard bits preventing carries between segments, and repair deterministically the explicitly bounded set of addresses where a guard fails.",
        mot("The construction first uses a constant number of cyclic shifts of whole chunk ranges, then corrects an explicitly bounded set of addresses.",
            "explicit", (1947, r"of cyclic shifts of whole chunk ranges, then corrects an explicitly bounded set of", r"addresses.")),
        [
            infer(r"For a Boolean control $c$, eight conditional $\pm1$ updates of two integers $(u,w)$ induce the cycle $(00\ 01\ 10)$ then $(01\ 10\ 11)$ on parities, which compose to $(00\ 10)(01\ 11)$.",
                  2032, r"If $c=0$, these operations do nothing.", r"$(00\ 10)(01\ 11)$."),
            infer(r"The net effect is that the parity of $u$ flips exactly when $c=1$, both quotients by two are preserved, and the arbitrary auxiliary integer $w$ is restored.",
                  2047, r"Thus the parity of $u$ is flipped precisely when $c=1$", r"is restored."),
            infer(r"Each coordinate moves by at most four from its start, so with ten guard values on either side no segment leaves its quotient range.",
                  2049, r"Each coordinate is updated four times by at most one, so its displacement",
                  r"The weaker bound eight will suffice below."),
            infer(r"Applied simultaneously to all segment pairs, each line is one cyclic shift of the target chunk by an offset independent of the target, hence a bijection of the rectangle.",
                  2066, r"Physically, a line updating $y$ by sign $\varepsilon$", r"bijection of the rectangle."),
            infer(r"Let $\mathcal B$ be the addresses where some used guard value $g$ fails $10\le g\le2^{K-1}-11$.",
                  2081, r"Let $\mathcal B$ consist of the addresses for which", r"2^{K-1}-11."),
            infer(r"Outside $\mathcal B$ every segment stays inside $[0,2^K-1]$ even after displacement eight, so no segment carries into or borrows from the next and the packed shifts agree with the independent XORs.",
                  2087, r"At an address outside $\mathcal B$, each segment initially lies in", r"into or borrows from the next segment."),
            infer(r"Each guard excludes exactly twenty of its $2^{K-1}$ values, so the union bound over $2(f-1)$ guards gives $|\mathcal B|\le\delta_{\rm bad}M$ with $\delta_{\rm bad}=\min\{1,80(f-1)2^{-K}\}$.",
                  2097, r"There are exactly twenty excluded values for each guard in", r"\delta_{\rm bad}M."),
            infer(r"The ideal map $T_0$ and the shift composition $S_0$ are bijections that agree off $\mathcal B$, so $T_0S_0^{-1}$ fixes $\mathcal B^c$ and permutes $\mathcal B$.",
                  2107, r"The ideal map $T_0$ preserves all guard values", r"permutes $\mathcal B$."),
            infer(r"Each rotation is conjugated by a chunk interchange moving its target after its two controls, costing $O(VL^\tau)$ with its padding and cleanup.",
                  2128, r"Pass only $L$ and these three positive canonical lengths to", r"padding and cleanup."),
            infer(r"The rotation itself is a controlled shift costing $O(V)$, its offset work absorbed by the $2^L$ nonempty target blocks per fibre.",
                  2149, r"therefore gives an $O(V)$ rotation", r"in each fiber."),
            infer(r"At most sixteen swaps and eight rotations make up $S_0$.",
                  2155, r"There are at most sixteen swaps and eight rotations in $S_0$."),
            infer(r"The exceptional addresses are found by a rank-counter scan costing $O(A_{\rm rect}^3)$ per record, and each destination $q'=T_0(S_0^{-1}(q))$ is computed by reversing the eight modular additions and applying the XORs.",
                  2157, r"To implement the correction, scan the stream with a record-rank counter.", r"those records."),
            infer(r"Stable binary radix sorting of only the extracted records by distinct destination keys, then one reinsertion scan, implements $T_0$ everywhere.",
                  2181, r"Hold the full stream untouched while stable binary radix sorting scans", r"This implements $T_0$ everywhere."),
            infer(r"The most significant selected position is handled by a direct two-bit XOR in $O(V)$; when $f=1$ this is the entire algorithm.",
                  2187, r"Finally apply the two-bit XOR to", r"this last step is the entire algorithm."),
            infer(r"The movement costs $O(V(L^\tau+1))$ and the repair adds terms bounded by $A_{\rm rect}^3/R$ and $80(f-1)2^{-K}A_{\rm rect}(1+A_{\rm rect}/R)$ after division by $V$, both $o(1)$ under the family bounds.",
                  2195, r"The movement costs $O(V(L^\tau+1))$.", r"This proves the final assertion."),
            infer(r"To add row $h'$ to row $h$ use slot $h'$ as $x$, slot $h$ as $y$ and any third slot as $z$; the lemma performs all the XORs $x_{h,j}\leftarrow x_{h,j}\oplus x_{h',j}$ at once and restores the third slot.",
                  2218, r"To add row $h'$ to row $h$, use slot $h'$ as $x$,", r"whatever its initial address value."),
        ],
        page="packed-selected-bit-addition",
        frm=["arbitrary-width-interchange", "controlled-shifts", "stream-operations", "fixed-tape-model", "binary-representation-and-bitwise-operations"],
        adds=[bind(r"f", "selected positions", "the number of selected bit positions, one per chunk, that are added at once", "count"),
              bind(r"L", "chunk width", "the width $fK$ in bits of a slot of $f$ chunks", "count")],
        salient="The third chunk may hold arbitrary data and is restored: the eight conditional updates compose to a single parity flip of $u$ with $w$ unchanged.")


def phase_frame_network():
    return show(
        r"The complex network over Gaussian dyadic values with binary labels and phase frames $\mathcal C_U=H^{\otimes m}\operatorname{diag}(i^{\operatorname{wt}(P_Ux)\bmod 4})H^{\otimes m}$ has each edge change equal to a product of two-point kernels $\tfrac{1+i}{2}I+\tfrac{1-i}{2}X_v$ and telescopes to $C^{\otimes m}$ on every role, with $s_{\rm c}<W_{\rm c}m$ kernel factors.",
        "Replace the permutation frames of the bit network by phase frames diagonalised by the common Hadamard transform, so that an orthogonal residual basis vector becomes one translation kernel.",
        mot("All frames are diagonalised by the same matrix $H^{\\otimes m}$, which turns phase differences into translation kernels.", "explicit",
            (1207, r"All frames are diagonalized by the same matrix $H^{\otimes m}$.")),
        [
            infer(r"Define $\mathcal C_U=H^{\otimes m}\operatorname{diag}_x(i^{q_U(x)})H^{\otimes m}$ with $q_U(x)=\operatorname{wt}(P_Ux)\bmod 4$.",
                  1193, r"\mathcal C_U=H^{\otimes m}\operatorname{diag}_x(i^{q_U(x)})H^{\otimes m}.", deferred=True),
            infer(r"The phase-frame difference on each edge is a product of one translation kernel $aI+bX_v$ (or its inverse) per vector of an orthonormal basis of the edge residual.",
                  1215, r"Its phase-frame difference", r"$(X_vf)(x)=f(x+v)$.", deferred=True),
            infer(r"The numbers of basis vectors sum to $s_{\rm c}=W_{\rm c}m-2N+2L_{\rm c}<W_{\rm c}m$.",
                  1221, r"The sum of these basis lengths is", r"<W_{\rm c}m.", deferred=True),
            infer(r"After source and sink diagonal corrections and undoing the bank exchange, telescoping the phase frames gives $C^{\otimes m}$ on every role, auxiliary roles included.",
                  1225, r"After source and sink diagonal corrections, and undoing the signed", r"including auxiliary roles.", deferred=True),
            infer(r"If $z\cdot w=0$ the intersection of supports is even, so the Hamming weight is additive modulo four.",
                  1238, r"If $z\cdot w=0$, the intersection cardinality is even, so weight is", r"additive modulo four.", deferred=True),
            infer(r"Each orthonormal vector $v$ has odd weight, so its phase factor $i^{\varepsilon[v\cdot x]}$ with $\varepsilon=\pm1$ equals $\frac{1+i^\varepsilon}{2}+\frac{1-i^\varepsilon}{2}(-1)^{v\cdot x}$, which $H\operatorname{diag}(1,-1)H=X$ turns into $aI+bX_v$ or its inverse.",
                  1246, r"Each $v$ has norm one, so $\operatorname{wt}(v)$ is odd.", r"becomes $aI+bX_v$ or its inverse.", deferred=True),
            infer(r"The pair $X_a\to Y_a$ is the exception: it carries $C^{-1}$ on the 27 coordinates of $\operatorname{supp}(u_a)$, which the identity $C=iZC^{-1}Z$ with sign corrections $Z_{a,k}$ and $i^{27k}$ turns back into $C$.",
                  1297, r"Since $2[u\cdot x]\equiv2\sum_{j\in\operatorname{supp}(u)}x_j", r"turns each of them into $C$.", deferred=True),
        ],
        page="phase-frame-network",
        frm=["frames", "rank-deficit", "subset-labels", "power-saving-recurrence", "linear-algebra-prelims", "butterfly", "complex-numbers"],
        salient="Every role, including all scratch roles, ends with the same operator $C^{\\otimes m}$, although the pair $X_a\\to Y_a$ passes through $C^{-1}$ on 27 coordinates that must be corrected back.")
